# Architecture + data path

Stack: Next.js App Router, Supabase Auth/DB/RLS/Realtime/Storage. No second backend.

Thread `communityId` through routes and queries. Browser = publishable key + RLS. Privileged admit/verify/album-password = server + service-role.

```ts
// ❌ unbounded, slow, leaky
await supabase.from('profiles').select('*')

// ✅ tenant + columns + page
await supabase.from('profiles')
  .select('id, username, avatar_url, status')
  .eq('community_id', communityId)
  .limit(PAGE)
```

## Layers

`src/app/` routes only · `src/features/<domain>/` queries, actions, components · `src/components/` domain-free UI (no service-role) · `src/lib/auth/dal.ts` membership + authz · `src/lib/supabase/` clients · `supabase/migrations/` schema, RLS, **and indexes in the same migration**. Full tree: `project-structure.mdc`.

## Must

- Every tenant table has `community_id` (or reaches it through chat membership).
- Dynamic fields: `profile_field_defs` (per community) + `profile_field_values` (typed columns — see performance.mdc).
- Messages carry a per-chat `seq`; unread is `chats.last_seq - memberships.last_read_seq`.
- Notes/ratings RLS: `author_id = (select auth.uid())`.
- Regenerate DB types after migrations.
- Stateless server code: no per-user state in module-level memory (sessions, Maps, rate limits). Instances are many and short-lived.
- Use `supabase-js` (HTTP, pooled by Supabase). Any direct Postgres client in serverless code must use the Supavisor transaction pooler, never the direct port.
